# Build Your First Flow

## Overview

A Flow is a validated graph of Action calls with one explicit output. This
Livebook builds one in two passes:

1. a two-step greeting, to learn how Steps, references, and `output` work; and
2. a checkout Flow that reuses the greeting and adds a Map, a Reduce, a
   Choice, and independent work that can run in parallel.

Read [Getting Started](getting-started.livemd) first if you have not defined
an Action yet. Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.12"}])
```

## Define The Flow

`input/1` reads Flow input. `result/2` reads a prior component result. The
result reference creates the dependency between the two Steps. Each `<-`
binds that data to a variable in the Step body.

```elixir
defmodule FirstFlow.Greeting do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "greeting",
    description: "Normalizes a name and creates a greeting",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{name: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{message: Zoi.string()})

  flow do
    step "normalize", name <- input(:name) do
      {:ok, %{name: name |> String.trim() |> String.capitalize()}}
    end

    step "greet", name <- result("normalize", :name) do
      {:ok, %{message: "Hello, " <> name <> "!"}}
    end

    output result("greet")
  end
end
```

Source order does not create a dependency. References and `needs:` create
dependencies. The body is normal Elixir code. Jido compiles it to an ordinary
Action; you do not need a separate Action module for each small operation.

## Inspect And Run

A Flow module exposes one canonical `%Jido.Flow{}` value.

```elixir
flow = FirstFlow.Greeting.flow()

{:ok, dependencies} = Jido.Flow.dependencies(flow)
dependencies["greet"]
```

Run the module or the canonical value.

```elixir
{:ok, %{message: "Hello, Ada!"}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.Greeting, %{name: "  ada  "})

{:ok, %{message: "Hello, Grace!"}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(flow, %{name: "grace"})
```

## Extract A Named Action When Needed

The Flow schemas above validate its input and output. An inline Step can
declare its own schemas and description through `inline:`. Extract a named
Action when a Step needs custom validation hooks or an independent public API.

```elixir
defmodule FirstFlow.CreateGreeting do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "create_greeting",
    description: "Creates one greeting",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{name: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{message: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{name: name}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{message: "Hello, " <> name <> "!"}}
  end
end

{:ok, %{message: "Hello, Ada!"}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.CreateGreeting, %{name: "Ada"})
```

In a Flow, replace the inline `greet` declaration with the existing Action
form:

```text
step "greet",
  action: FirstFlow.CreateGreeting,
  params: %{name: result("normalize", :name)}
```

## Grow The Flow

A real Flow does more than call two Steps in a row. The next Flow prices an
order. It greets the customer, prices each order line, adds the lines, picks a
discount, and builds a receipt. Along the way it uses:

| Component | What it does here |
| --- | --- |
| Nested Flow | A `step` whose target is `FirstFlow.Greeting` runs that whole Flow. |
| `map` | Prices each order line with the same Action. |
| `reduce` | Adds the line totals, one at a time, into a subtotal. |
| `choice` | Picks a 10 percent discount for large orders, or none. |
| Inline `step` | Combines several results into a receipt. |

### Define The Actions

Map, Reduce, and Choice call Action modules, not inline bodies. Each Action
validates its own input and output.

```elixir
defmodule FirstFlow.LineTotal do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "line_total",
    description: "Prices one order line",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        sku: Zoi.string(),
        price: Zoi.integer(),
        quantity: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(1)
      }),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{sku: Zoi.string(), total: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{sku: sku, price: price, quantity: quantity}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{sku: sku, total: price * quantity}}
  end
end

defmodule FirstFlow.AddLine do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "add_line",
    description: "Adds one line total to a running subtotal",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{subtotal: Zoi.integer(), line_total: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{subtotal: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{subtotal: subtotal, line_total: line_total}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{subtotal: subtotal + line_total}}
  end
end

defmodule FirstFlow.ApplyDiscount do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "apply_discount",
    description: "Applies a percentage discount to a subtotal",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{subtotal: Zoi.integer(), percent: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema:
      Zoi.object(%{subtotal: Zoi.integer(), discount: Zoi.integer(), total: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{subtotal: subtotal, percent: percent}, _context) do
    discount = div(subtotal * percent, 100)
    {:ok, %{subtotal: subtotal, discount: discount, total: subtotal - discount}}
  end
end
```

### Compose The Checkout

Prices are integers in cents.

```elixir
defmodule FirstFlow.Checkout do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "checkout",
    description: "Greets a customer and prices an order",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        customer: Zoi.string(),
        items:
          Zoi.list(
            Zoi.object(%{sku: Zoi.string(), price: Zoi.integer(), quantity: Zoi.integer()})
          )
      })

  flow do
    step "greeting",
      action: FirstFlow.Greeting,
      params: %{name: input(:customer)}

    map "lines",
      collection: input(:items),
      action: FirstFlow.LineTotal,
      params: %{sku: item(:sku), price: item(:price), quantity: item(:quantity)}

    reduce "subtotal" do
      collection result("lines")
      initial %{subtotal: 0}
      action FirstFlow.AddLine
      params %{subtotal: accumulator(:subtotal), line_total: item(:total)}
    end

    choice "pricing" do
      option "bulk",
        condition: result("subtotal", :subtotal) >= 5_000,
        action: FirstFlow.ApplyDiscount,
        params: %{subtotal: result("subtotal", :subtotal), percent: 10}

      otherwise action: FirstFlow.ApplyDiscount,
                params: %{subtotal: result("subtotal", :subtotal), percent: 0}
    end

    step "receipt",
         [
           greeting <- result("greeting", :message),
           lines <- result("lines"),
           pricing <- result("pricing")
         ] do
      {:ok,
       %{
         message: greeting,
         line_count: length(lines),
         subtotal: pricing.subtotal,
         discount: pricing.discount,
         total: pricing.total
       }}
    end

    output result("receipt")
  end
end
```

Read each component from its references:

- `"greeting"` passes `input(:customer)` to the greeting Flow. Its result is
  that Flow's output, `%{message: ...}`.
- `"lines"` runs `FirstFlow.LineTotal` once per item. `item(:price)` reads a
  field of the current item. The result is a list in item order.
- `"subtotal"` starts from `initial` and calls `FirstFlow.AddLine` for each
  line. `accumulator(:subtotal)` reads the running value. The result is the
  final accumulator.
- `"pricing"` checks its options in order. The condition is a data-only
  expression, not Elixir code. When no option matches, `otherwise` runs.
- `"receipt"` binds three results in one list and builds the output in normal
  Elixir.

### Run It

A large order gets the discount:

```elixir
order = %{
  customer: "  ada ",
  items: [
    %{sku: "book", price: 2_400, quantity: 2},
    %{sku: "pen", price: 150, quantity: 4}
  ]
}

{:ok, receipt} = Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.Checkout, order)
receipt
```

A small order takes the `otherwise` path:

```elixir
{:ok, %{discount: 0, total: 300}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.Checkout, %{
    customer: "grace",
    items: [%{sku: "pen", price: 150, quantity: 2}]
  })
```

### See What Can Run In Parallel

`Jido.Flow.dependencies/1` lists what each component waits for:

```elixir
{:ok, dependencies} = Jido.Flow.dependencies(FirstFlow.Checkout.flow())
Map.new(dependencies, fn {name, info} -> {name, info.effective} end)
```

`"greeting"` and `"lines"` wait for nothing, so they can run at the same
time. The items inside `"lines"` are independent too. `"subtotal"`,
`"pricing"`, and `"receipt"` must wait for the results they read.

Exec runs one unit of work at a time by default. Pass `max_concurrency:` to
run independent work in parallel. The result is the same:

```elixir
{:ok, ^receipt} = Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.Checkout, order, %{}, max_concurrency: 4)
```

### Read A Failure

When an Action rejects its input, the error names the component and the
item that failed. Here, the second line has a quantity of `0`:

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FirstFlow.Checkout, %{
    customer: "ada",
    items: [
      %{sku: "book", price: 2_400, quantity: 1},
      %{sku: "pen", price: 150, quantity: 0}
    ]
  })

{Exception.message(error), Map.take(error.details, [:node, :item_index])}
```

Components that depend on `"lines"` do not run, and the Flow returns no
partial result. See
[Errors](errors.md) for every error shape.

## What You Learned

- A Flow is a graph of named components. References such as `input/1`,
  `result/2`, and `item/1` connect them and create the dependencies.
- `output` selects the value that the Flow returns.
- A `step` can call an Action, a whole Flow, or an inline body.
- `map`, `reduce`, and `choice` call Action modules for collections and
  decisions.
- Independent work runs in parallel when you pass `max_concurrency:`, with the
  same result.

